      {
        "id": "cv-evaluation",
        "role": "about",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"conclusion\",1]",
          "anchor": "conclusion",
          "quote": "In each of the four cases, the measurement we relied on differed from the deployed system: in the detector head, padding and downscaling; in the width of the spines in an early OCR test; in whether the dictionary contained the answer; and in whether the page ran on a desktop or a phone.",
          "hash": "47f4bf8157796d27dc2d41a91e3aaddebd6183d16f05d1775f86a39f2417bfa2"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "object-detection",
        "role": "applies",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",2]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "A photo is first downscaled so that its long side is at most 1,600 px. A one-class oriented bounding box (OBB) detector finds the spines. Near-duplicate boxes are merged (Section 3.5), and the number of boxes scoring 0.4 or more is the book count. Each box is then cut out with a margin of 8% of its short side, rotated so that its long side is vertical, and upscaled if its short side is below 96 px.",
          "hash": "47a6b7f2df9dece4417da0ca5bd70c47f8e2d281e5d4511a6cf0cf29c50c19b1"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "korean-ocr",
        "role": "applies",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",3]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "Inside the crop, PP-OCRv5 finds text lines and the Korean PP-OCRv5 recognizer reads them; the reading direction is decided from recognition confidence (Section 4.3). Lines that match one of 34 publisher names are dropped, and the line with the tallest glyphs becomes the title query.",
          "hash": "a8d828d5c1c0da0b9670c987b8309d854a739d49ffcb0ca6be86de1367bca67f"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "keyword-search",
        "role": "applies",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",4]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "Matching normalizes the query, retrieves 50 candidates from a 50,000-book dictionary with character 2-gram and 3-gram BM25, and rescores them with string similarity plus a few rules (Section 5). Each spine ends in one of three states: confirmed (the dictionary entry is taken as the answer), review (candidates are shown and the user picks), or unmatched.",
          "hash": "42e4da97f352cf20759e335c63c692349941e96920844bb8ef223bed57187f05"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "reranking",
        "role": "applies",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",4]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "Matching normalizes the query, retrieves 50 candidates from a 50,000-book dictionary with character 2-gram and 3-gram BM25, and rescores them with string similarity plus a few rules (Section 5). Each spine ends in one of three states: confirmed (the dictionary entry is taken as the answer), review (candidates are shown and the user picks), or unmatched.",
          "hash": "42e4da97f352cf20759e335c63c692349941e96920844bb8ef223bed57187f05"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "browser-inference",
        "role": "applies",
        "evidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",10]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "All three models run in ONNX Runtime Web 1.31.1 on the multithreaded WebAssembly backend inside a Web Worker. The app is a static site made of plain ES modules with no build step. Besides serving its files, the server has one optional endpoint, which forwards the title text of unconfirmed spines to a book-search API when the user asks for it (Section 5.5).",
          "hash": "7a57d838d048aaf0a034573cbe636dfd3cf21e078ee486caf9a6dbacb9ef48f9"
        }
      }
